        "mocha's command-line parser (parseMochaArgs in lib/cli/parse-args.js) accepts each one-character option alias written with one dash, but the same alias written with two dashes, with its value given as the next argument, is not read as that option. Today parseMochaArgs([\"--t\", \"0\"], { timeout: 1000 }) throws \"Mocha flag '--t' given invalid option: '0'\" (so mocha --t 0 a.spec.cjs exits 1 with that error), parseMochaArgs([\"--R\", \"dot\"], { timeout: 1000 }) gives reporter true and takes \"dot\" as a spec file (_ is [\"dot\"]), and [\"--r\", \"./x.js\", \"--r\", \"./y.js\"] gives require [\"true\"] with both files in _. A one-character alias written with two dashes must mean exactly what it means with one dash, with the value given as the next argument or after \"=\". With defaults { timeout: 1000 }: [\"--t\", \"0\"] and [\"--t=0\"] must both give timeout \"0\" and _ []; [\"--R\", \"dot\"] must give reporter \"dot\" and _ []; [\"--r\", \"./x.js\", \"--r\", \"./y.js\"] must give require [\"./x.js\", \"./y.js\"] and _ []; [\"--j\", \"3\"] must give jobs 3; [\"test/a.js\", \"--t\", \"0\"] must give _ [\"test/a.js\"] and timeout \"0\"; [\"--timeout\", \"5\", \"--t\", \"7\"] must give timeout \"7\"; and, as with one dash, the alias itself must not appear as a key of the result (no t, R, r or j property). What must not change: one-dash aliases ([\"-t\", \"0\"] gives timeout \"0\"; [\"-R\", \"json\", \"-b\"] gives reporter \"json\" and bail true), the \"=\" form that already works today ([\"--t=0\"] gives timeout \"0\"), long names and long aliases ([\"--timeouts=200\"] gives timeout \"200\", [\"--reporter-options=a=b\"] gives reporter-option [\"a=b\"]), negation ([\"--no-bail\"] gives bail false), boolean one-character aliases that already work with two dashes ([\"--b\"] gives bail true, [\"--A\"] gives async-only true) and unknown flags ([\"--x\"] still gives x true). The parser is ESM in lib/cli/parse-args.js, where option names are normalised before yargs-parser reads them. Add regression tests for this behaviour to the project's existing test suite (the CLI's unit tests are under test/node-unit/cli/). The unit tests run with npm run test-node:unit (mocha under nyc over test/unit and test/node-unit).\n\nVisible acceptance check (it must pass; it is not the only criterion): node acceptance/visible/check.mjs\nThe project's own tests run with: npm run test-node:unit\nDeliverables, all required; the task is not done until each is in the tree:\n1. The implementation of the behaviour described above, in the project's own source.\n2. Regression tests: at least one new test case, in the project's own test directory and style, that fails on the current code and passes with your change, adding at least one new assertion. A change without a new test is incomplete.\n3. The visible acceptance check (node acceptance/visible/check.mjs) and the project's own test command (npm run test-node:unit) both passing on the final tree.\nAllowed changes: implementation code, and regression tests for the change (new test files, or new cases added to existing test files, in the project's own test directory and style).\nDo not modify anything under acceptance/visible/.\nDo not change how the project's checks run: leave the test and coverage configuration files (.taprc*, .nycrc*, .c8rc*, .mocharc*, jest.config.*, vitest.config.*, ava.config.*, karma.conf.*) and the test, pretest and posttest scripts and runner sections of package.json as they are.\nDo not delete or weaken existing tests: keep every existing assertion (it may move to another test file), add no skip, only or todo markers, and add no coverage-ignore pragmas. A change that does any of these is rejected at evaluation whatever else passes.",
